@* BRF-046 (RF029, RF030) — the SLA and overdue dashboard.
   Route note: /sla/atrasos is a literal no controller can claim — SlaRulesController owns "sla-rules" and
   nothing routes under "sla/" (the USR-042 AmbiguousMatchException trap).
   The policy is any-of: the seeded Gestor de Design and Administrador hold both view-all permissions, but a
   custom role may hold one, and it must still reach the half it can read. Each panel gates itself on its own
   permission, so the other simply does not render. *@
@page "/sla/atrasos"
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = "tasks.view-all,briefings.view-all")]
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization

<PageTitle>Atrasos e SLA</PageTitle>

<div class="min-h-full bg-[#090909] px-4 py-8 text-white sm:px-8">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1100px]">
        <div>
            <h1 class="text-[32px] font-medium leading-[1.13] tracking-[-1px] text-white">Atrasos e SLA</h1>
            <p class="mt-1 text-[15px] leading-[1.3] tracking-[-0.15px] text-[#999999]">
                Tarefas e briefings que passaram do prazo, conforme as regras de SLA em vigor.
            </p>
        </div>

        @* The page composes and holds no state: each panel owns its own load, paging and failure, so one
           failing read never blanks the other. *@
        <div class="mt-[30px] flex flex-col gap-[20px]">
            <OverdueTasksPanel />
            <OverdueBriefingsPanel />
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
